00:10:56.029 --> 00:10:59.679
Now, there's not an investment banker that would get out of

00:10:59.699 --> 00:11:00.699
bed for

00:11:00.699 --> 00:11:04.199
50,000 bucks for two months or for a month but it was $50,

00:11:04.179 --> 00:11:05.179
000 greater

00:11:05.179 --> 00:11:08.679
than zero, which is what I had earned so far as the head of

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:09.110
the

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:10.110
Russian investment banking business.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:10.310
&gt;&gt;

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:11.110
[Laughing]

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:12.759
BILL B.: And so I took the assignment.

00:11:12.779 --> 00:11:16.279
And I got on a plane to Murmansk and I got to Murmansk which

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:17.120
is a

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:20.149
very, very desolate, terrible place.

00:11:20.169 --> 00:11:23.859
Really just a horrible place.

00:11:23.879 --> 00:11:24.079
&gt;&gt;

00:11:24.079 --> 00:11:25.079
[Laughing]

00:11:24.079 --> 00:11:27.710
BILL B.: And I sat down with the head of the fishing fleet

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:28.730
and I

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:30.779
asked him, what's going on?

00:11:30.799 --> 00:11:32.370
How could I help?

00:11:32.389 --> 00:11:34.139
And he walked me through the math.

00:11:33.969 --> 00:11:37.029
They basically had a hundred ships, each ship costs $20

00:11:37.049 --> 00:11:38.049
million new,

00:11:38.049 --> 00:11:42.120
so there's $2 billion worth of ships, maybe seven years old

00:11:42.139 --> 00:11:43.139
and total

00:11:43.139 --> 00:11:45.639
so about half depreciated, so a billion dollars worth of

