had done

00:18:33.339 --> 00:18:34.339
in Russia.

00:18:33.839 --> 00:18:36.839
So I go to these meetings and I take them through my

00:18:36.329 --> 00:18:38.000
PowerPoint and

00:18:38.019 --> 00:18:41.019
every single one of them says at the end of the meeting, "

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:41.490
That's the

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:43.490
most amazing thing that I've ever heard.

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:45.240
Can we give you some money to manage?"

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:48.240
And I said, well, we're not actually in the business of

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:48.240
managing

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:49.240
people's money.

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:51.990
Let me go back and ask my colleagues and see whether we can

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:52.490
do this.

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:56.049
So I get back and I ask my boss in London if this is

00:18:56.069 --> 00:18:57.069
something that we

00:18:57.069 --> 00:18:58.319
can do for other people.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:18:58.940
He said, great idea.

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:00.059
For sure.

00:19:00.079 --> 00:19:02.329
Let's form a task force to study it.

00:19:01.359 --> 00:19:01.559
&gt;&gt;

00:19:01.559 --> 00:19:02.559
[Laughing]

00:19:01.559 --> 00:19:05.889
BILL B.: So he said, next Monday will be the first task

00:19:05.909 --> 00:19:06.909
force

00:19:06.909 --> 00:19:07.909
meeting.

00:19:07.159 --> 00:19:10.659
So I show up to the task force meeting and it's like this

00:19:09.659 --> 00:19:10.659
room.

00:19:10.659 --> 00:19:13.659
There's like 50 people showed up for the first task force

00:19:12.629 --> 00:19:13.629
meeting and

00:19:13.629 --> 00:19:16.629
there's only, like, two people in the firm who knew anything

00:19:15.859 --> 00:19:16.859
about

00:19:16.859 --> 00:19:18.339
Russia.

00:19:18.359 --> 00:19:21.109
And I look around the room and people -- and there was,

00:19:20.779 --> 00:19:21.779
like,

00:19:21.789 --> 00:19:24.920
directors, managing directors, and senior managing

00:19:24.940 --> 00:19:26.450
directors, and I

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:28.970
was like, some junior vice president, and I was looking

00:19:28.289 --> 00:19:29.289
around the

00:19:29.289 --> 00:19:32.039
room, right, and all these guys were sort of arguing with

00:19:31.539 --> 00:19:32.539
each other

00:19:32.539 --> 00:19:35.539
about which part of the firm was gonna get the credit for

00:19:35.289 --> 00:19:36.289
this and who

00:19:36.289 --> 00:19:37.539
was gonna get the money.

00:19:37.539 --> 00:19:40.539
And I was looking around thinking to myself I know there's

00:19:40.039 --> 00:19:41.039
one thing I

00:19:41.039 --> 00:19:44.039
know for sure who's not gonna get any money outta this

00:19:43.039 --> 00:19:44.039
thing.

00:19:44.039 --> 00:19:44.240
&gt;&gt;

00:19:44.039 --> 00:19:45.039
[Laughing]

00:19:44.039 --> 00:19:51.379
BILL B.: So I got really angry and I went back and I was

00:19:51.399 --> 00:19:52.399
tossing and

00:19:52.399 --> 00:19:56.149
turning for a few days and I just -- I said, I just can't do

00:19:55.349 --> 00:19:56.349
this.

00:19:56.349 --> 00:20:00.099
And so I sucked up my gut, and I went into the head of the

00:19:59.349 --> 00:20:00.349
trading

00:20:00.349 --> 00:20:02.099
floor and I said, I'm quitting.

00:20:01.849 --> 00:20:04.599
I'm gonna set up my own fund to do this.

00:20:04.349 --> 00:20:05.849
He said, Bill, you're crazy.

00:20:05.599 --> 00:20:07.099
You have such a promising career.

00:20:07.099 --> 00:20:08.599
And I said, I'm sorry.

00:20:08.349 --> 00:20:10.849
I'm setting up my own fund to do this.

00:20:10.599 --> 00:20:13.849
And I went -- I left Salomon Brothers and I set up my fund

00:20:13.349 --> 00:20:14.349
called the

00:20:14.349 --> 00:20:15.349
Hermitage Fund.

00:20:14.849 --> 00:20:18.599
And I went back to all of these famous guys who I had met on

00:20:17.849 --> 00:20:18.849
Wall

00:20:18.849 --> 00:20:22.099
Street and I said, would you be willing to invest with me in

00:20:21.349 --> 00:20:22.349
my

00:20:22.349 --> 00:20:23.349
Hermitage Fund?

00:20:22.849 --> 00:20:26.349
And one of the guys who I had met was a very famous, now

00:20:25.849 --> 00:20:26.849
deceased man,

00:20:26.849 --> 00:20:29.349
named Edmond Safra, who was the owner of Republic National

00:20:29.099 --> 00:20:30.099
Bank in New

00:20:30.099 --> 00:20:31.099
York.

00:20:30.349 --> 00:20:33.599
And in the world of private banking his name was as good as

00:20:33.099 --> 00:20:34.099
it ever --

00:20:34.099 --> 00:20:36.099
his name is a legend in private banking.

00:20:36.099 --> 00:20:39.099
You might not know about him now, but if you knew the

00:20:38.599 --> 00:20:39.599
history of

00:20:39.599 --> 00:20:42.099
wealth and private banking this man was just like gold.

00:20:42.099 --> 00:20:45.099
And Edmond Safra put up the first $25 million to be my

00:20:44.349 --> 00:20:45.349
anchor

00:20:45.349 --> 00:20:46.349
investor.

00:20:45.599 --> 00:20:48.599
We became joint venture partners and he said, if you do a

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:49.240
good job

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:52.240
with this 25 million then I will introduce you to all the

00:20:51.589 --> 00:20:52.589
rich people

00:20:52.589 --> 00:20:55.539
in the world and will make you a spectacular success.

00:20:55.559 --> 00:20:57.980
But you node to do a good job first.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:01.809
So I moved to Moscow from London in 1996 and started

00:21:01.829 --> 00:21:02.829
investing.

00:21:02.829 --> 00:21:07.470
And it was really a very interesting thing because in 1996

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:08.490
there was

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:13.129
not a single Wall Street-educated principal investor who was

00:21:13.149 --> 00:21:14.149
actually

00:21:14.149 --> 00:21:15.649
sitting on the ground in Russia.

00:21:15.649 --> 00:21:18.359
There was a lot of Wall Street principal investors sitting

00:21:18.379 --> 00:21:19.379
on the

00:21:19.379 --> 00:21:22.379
ground in Wall Street and there was a lot of Wall Street-

00:21:22.259 --> 00:21:23.559
educated

00:21:23.579 --> 00:21:27.170
brokers sitting on the ground in Moscow, but no investors.

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:29.690
And it was very interesting 'cause the brokers would all

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:30.220
write

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:32.779
research about things that they could make a lot of

00:21:32.799 --> 00:21:33.799
commissions buying

00:21:33.799 --> 00:21:36.559
and selling and they wouldn't write research about the

00:21:36.579 --> 00:21:37.579
things that

00:21:37.579 --> 00:21:38.579
made the most sense.

00:21:38.429 --> 00:21:40.929
And basically you end up in a situation where anything --

00:21:40.809 --> 00:21:41.809
any stock

00:21:41.809 --> 00:21:44.559
that was researched by the brokers would trade at ten times

00:21:44.269 --> 00:21:45.269
the

00:21:45.269 --> 00:21:48.019
valuation of any stock in the same industry that wasn't

00:21:47.899 --> 00:21:48.899
researched by

00:21:48.899 --> 00:21:51.649
the brokers and nobody had had any confidence in buying the

00:21:51.179 --> 00:21:52.179
stuff that

00:21:52.179 --> 00:21:54.679
wasn't researched because they couldn't do any research.

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:56.220
So, again, this is not rocket science.

00:21:56.129 --> 00:21:58.210
This is just simple stuff.

00:21:58.230 --> 00:22:00.480
I thought, well, here I am on the ground.

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:01.480
I could do my own research.

00:22:01.059 --> 00:22:04.559
Why don't I go visit the company this trades at 1/10 the

00:22:03.119 --> 00:22:04.119
valuation of

00:22:04.119 --> 00:22:06.369
the other one where there's a research report?

00:22:06.119 --> 00:22:09.369
So I went and visited the oil company that traited at 1/10

00:22:09.109 --> 00:22:10.109
the

00:22:10.109 --> 00:22:13.899
valuation of Lukoil and a couple of them at did and I rooked

00:22:13.919 --> 00:22:14.919
at them

00:22:14.919 --> 00:22:17.919
and there was no difference between the ones that were 1/10

00:22:16.919 --> 00:22:17.919
the

00:22:17.919 --> 00:22:20.669
valuation except for the fact that there wasn't a credit

00:22:19.669 --> 00:22:20.669
Swiss

00:22:20.669 --> 00:22:21.669
research report.

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:23.740
So after going through these companies and realizing that

00:22:22.629 --> 00:22:23.629
they're

00:22:23.629 --> 00:22:26.129
exactly the same, they had the same surley management, the

00:22:25.299 --> 00:22:26.299
same

00:22:26.299 --> 00:22:30.500
rusting oil derricks, the same bad tax inspectors, the same

00:22:30.519 --> 00:22:33.720
everything, one was ten times cheaper than the other.

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:36.740
And, by the way, the one that was ten times more expensive

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:37.240
was still

00:22:37.240 --> 00:22:39.490
1/10 the valuation of the western oil companies.

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:42.240
These are trading at 1/100 of the valuation of the western

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:42.240
oil

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:43.240
companies.

00:22:42.490 --> 00:22:45.490
So I decided I'm gonna invest in the stuff that's

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:45.490
unresearched 'cause

00:22:45.490 --> 00:22:46.990
I can do my own research.

00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:50.240
So I did my own research, invested in them, and my fund went

00:22:49.689 --> 00:22:50.689
up in the

00:22:50.689 --> 00:22:53.769
first month of operations it went up 35 percent.

00:22:53.789 --> 00:22:55.789
Not in a year but in a month.

00:22:55.419 --> 00:23:00.359
So some of Edmond Safra's clients had heard about this new

00:23:00.379 --> 00:23:01.379
hot thing

00:23:01.379 --> 00:23:04.379
in Russia and they called Edmond and said, can we invest in

00:23:04.259 --> 00:23:05.259
your fund?

00:23:05.259 --> 00:23:06.759
He said, no, no, no, no.

00:23:06.759 --> 00:23:08.259
We don't know this guy.

00:23:08.009 --> 00:23:09.009
He could be stupid.

00:23:09.009 --> 00:23:10.259
He could be a crook.

00:23:10.259 --> 00:23:11.259
I have no idea.

00:23:11.259 --> 00:23:14.259
I wanna audit -- I wanna have, like, a year's track record,

00:23:13.259 --> 00:23:14.259
audits,

00:23:14.259 --> 00:23:17.509
everything, and then I'll let everybody in but I don't wanna

00:23:16.509 --> 00:23:17.509
put my

00:23:17.509 --> 00:23:20.849
name next to his until I know he's a solid guy.

00:23:20.869 --> 00:23:22.869
The next month we were up 40 percent.

00:23:22.409 --> 00:23:22.609
&gt;&gt;

00:23:22.609 --> 00:23:23.609
[Laughing]

00:23:22.609 --> 00:23:25.680
BILL B.: And the guys who had called him and said, Edmond,

00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:26.700
what are

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:27.700
you trying to do?

00:23:27.700 --> 00:23:30.700
That was 40 percent we would have made if we had invested.

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:32.450
And people were getting angry with him.

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:35.200
They said, listen, Edmond, we're gonna take some of our

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:35.700
money out of

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:38.950
your bank if you're being so greedy and not letting us in

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:39.200
this thing,

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:40.450
just keeping it for yourself.

00:23:40.250 --> 00:23:40.450
&gt;&gt;

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:41.450
[Laughing]

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:43.200
BILL B.: And he couldn't have imagined that his business

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:43.700
risk was not

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:46.200
gonna be me blowing up, but me doing too well.

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:48.700
All of these people were threatening to leave his bank

00:23:48.450 --> 00:23:49.450
because I had

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:51.950
done so well and he wouldn't let 'em in.

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:54.450
And so he relented, opened up the fund to outside investors,

00:23:53.950 --> 00:23:54.950
and by

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:58.200
the end of the first year we had about a $100 million under

00:23:57.450 --> 00:23:58.450
management.

00:23:58.450 --> 00:24:00.450
We were up, like, a hundred 50 percent.

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:02.200
The next year it was even better.

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:05.450
We were up 200 -- I think we were up 242 percent in 1997.

00:24:05.450 --> 00:24:10.009
We were up 800 percent from when we had launched 18 months

00:24:10.029 --> 00:24:11.549
earlier.

00:24:11.569 --> 00:24:14.319
My fund was over a billion dollars -- and again, this was

00:24:13.639 --> 00:24:14.639
when a

00:24:14.639 --> 00:24:15.889
billion dollars really meant something.

00:24:15.689 --> 00:24:15.889
&gt;&gt;

00:24:15.889 --> 00:24:16.889
[Laughing]

00:24:15.889 --> 00:24:18.889
BILL B.: -- and I was the largest investor by any stretch of

00:24:18.819 --> 00:24:19.819
the

00:24:19.819 --> 00:24:21.549
imagination in a small market.

00:24:21.569 --> 00:24:24.319
They were writing articles about how clever I was ton front

00:24:23.799 --> 00:24:24.799
page of

00:24:24.799 --> 00:24:26.720
the New York times.

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:28.740
My clients were inviting me to their yachts.

00:24:28.409 --> 00:24:28.609
&gt;&gt;

00:24:28.609 --> 00:24:29.609
[Laughing]

00:24:28.609 --> 00:24:33.569
BILL B.: I was in my early 30s's.

00:24:33.589 --> 00:24:38.809
I thought, I've just figured it all out.

00:24:38.829 --> 00:24:39.029
&gt;&gt;

00:24:39.029 --> 00:24:40.029
[Laughing]

00:24:39.029 --> 00:24:41.259
BILL B.: And I had no perspective.

00:24:41.279 --> 00:24:44.279
Everything that I've just told you was like -- one of them

00:24:43.269 --> 00:24:44.269
by

00:24:44.269 --> 00:24:47.269
themselves might not have been a sell signal but a young guy

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:47.970
in his

00:24:47.970 --> 00:24:51.220
30's with the biggest fund in the market up 800 percent in

00:24:50.409 --> 00:24:51.409
18 months,

00:24:51.409 --> 00:24:53.909
clients and yachts, front page articles, that was a sell

00:24:53.539 --> 00:24:54.539
signal if

00:24:54.539 --> 00:24:55.539
there ever was one.

00:24:55.539 --> 00:24:55.740
&gt;&gt;

00:24:55.539 --> 00:24:56.539
[Laughing]

00:24:55.539 --> 00:25:00.559
BILL B.: But I was too young and too inexperienced to

00:25:00.579 --> 00:25:01.579
understand

00:25:01.579 --> 00:25:02.579
that.

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:01.920
&gt;&gt;

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:02.720
[Laughing]

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:10.569
BILL B.: In 1997 the Asian currency started to devalue,

00:25:10.589 --> 00:25:11.589
Asian markets

00:25:11.589 --> 00:25:15.159
started to go down, the Russian government had a huge debt

00:25:15.179 --> 00:25:16.179
burden, it

00:25:16.179 --> 00:25:19.179
was rolling on a three-month basis with hedge funds and rich

00:25:18.899 --> 00:25:20.690
guys and

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:22.210
they couldn't roll it over.

00:25:21.889 --> 00:25:24.409
Russia defaulted, devalued, the stock market went down 80

00:25:24.429 --> 00:25:25.429
percent, my

00:25:25.429 --> 00:25:29.409
1 billion went down to a hundred million.

00:25:29.429 --> 00:25:31.429
There was no more yacht invitations after that.

00:25:31.059 --> 00:25:31.259
&gt;&gt;

00:25:31.259 --> 00:25:32.259
[Laughing]

00:25:31.259 --> 00:25:35.980
BILL B.: But then I discovered something far more disturbing

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:37.000
than

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:39.750
losing 90 percent of your money which was that the companies

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:40.740
I had

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:43.240
invested in which were basically oil and gas companies in

00:25:42.740 --> 00:25:43.740
Russia were

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:47.210
run by people who you've now -- who are now sort of properly

00:25:47.230 --> 00:25:48.859
immortalized.

00:25:48.879 --> 00:25:50.099
The Russian oligarchs.

00:25:50.119 --> 00:25:53.960
This small group of about 22 guys basically owned a majority

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:54.980
of all

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:56.269
these companies.

00:25:56.289 --> 00:25:59.289
And they used to behave a little bit when they thought that

00:25:59.259 --> 00:26:00.259
they had

00:26:00.259 --> 00:26:02.740
access to western capital, when the western capital markets

00:26:02.759 --> 00:26:03.869
were open.

00:26:03.889 --> 00:26:07.690
But after the Russian economy defaulted and devalued, and

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:08.710
went to

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:11.710
hell, there was no more western investors in any case and so

00:26:11.519 --> 00:26:12.519
they no

00:26:12.519 --> 00:26:16.319
longer had any need to behave themselves.

00:26:16.339 --> 00:26:20.720
And so in 1998 and 1999 the Russian oligarchs embarked on an

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:21.740
orgy of

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:23.990
stealing that's been unprecedented in the history of

00:26:23.649 --> 00:26:24.649
business.

00:26:24.649 --> 00:26:27.740
I mean, it's just remarkable every type of scam you could

00:26:27.759 --> 00:26:28.759
ever

00:26:28.759 --> 00:26:30.460
imagine, they were trying to do.

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:33.119
Asset stripping, transfer pricing, delusion, embezzlement,

00:26:33.139 --> 00:26:34.139
you name

00:26:34.139 --> 00:26:35.389
it, they were doing it.

00:26:35.189 --> 00:26:38.189
And I was owning one or 2 percent of these companies and

00:26:37.839 --> 00:26:38.839
just watching

00:26:38.839 --> 00:26:43.019
all the money that the companies had just disappearing.

00:26:43.039 --> 00:26:45.089
And so I had to decide something.

00:26:45.109 --> 00:26:48.359
I was either gonna stay there and just put up with all this

00:26:48.169 --> 00:26:49.759
stuff or I

00:26:49.779 --> 00:26:55.480
was gonna have to -- I mean, basically there was really only

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:56.500
two

00:26:56.500 --> 00:26:57.500
choices.

00:26:56.899 --> 00:26:58.899
You could either leave or you could fight.

00:26:58.799 --> 00:27:03.159
I could not just watch it happen.

00:27:03.179 --> 00:27:05.009
And so we decided to fight.

00:27:05.029 --> 00:27:09.759
And the most famous fight involved Gazprom.

00:27:09.779 --> 00:27:12.779
Gazprom was a company that nobody had really heard of in the

00:27:12.369 --> 00:27:13.369
west

00:27:13.369 --> 00:27:15.379
until, like, ten years ago.

00:27:15.399 --> 00:27:18.980
And Gazprom, it's biggest gas company in the world.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:22.000
It's about ten times the size of Exxon in terms of

00:27:21.909 --> 00:27:22.909
hydrocarbon

00:27:22.909 --> 00:27:24.179
reserves.

00:27:24.199 --> 00:27:30.190
And Gazprom, in 1999, was trading at a 99.7 percent discount

00:27:30.210 --> 00:27:31.210
to BP or

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:35.450
Exxon per barrel of hydrocarbon reserves.

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:38.220
Why was it at such a big discount in because everybody

00:27:37.449 --> 00:27:38.449
thought that

00:27:38.449 --> 00:27:42.589
everything was being stolen out of Gazprom.

00:27:42.609 --> 00:27:45.859
So I looked at this thing and I said to myself, could they

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:46.720
really be

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:47.970
stealing everything out of Gazprom?

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:49.970
That would be just the most remarkable thing.

00:27:49.970 --> 00:27:52.470
A company ten times the size of Exxon everything being

00:27:52.470 --> 00:27:53.879
stolen.

00:27:53.899 --> 00:27:57.669
So I got together with my team and I said, let's do a

00:27:57.689 --> 00:27:58.689
stealing

00:27:58.689 --> 00:28:01.799
analysis of Gazprom.

00:28:01.819 --> 00:28:04.819
And they looked at me, how do you do a stealing analysis?

00:28:04.279 --> 00:28:04.480
&gt;&gt;

00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:05.480
[Laughing]

00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:07.480
BILL B.: So we started thinking, well, how to you do a

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:07.730
stealing

00:28:07.730 --> 00:28:08.730
analysis?

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:12.269
And in the introduction they didn't teach me this at

00:28:12.289 --> 00:28:13.289
Stanford Business

00:28:13.289 --> 00:28:15.639
School.

00:28:15.659 --> 00:28:18.909
You couldn't go to the company and say, "How much are you

00:28:16.649 --> 00:28:17.649
stealing" --

00:28:17.649 --> 00:28:17.849
&gt;&gt;

00:28:17.649 --> 00:28:18.649
[Laughing]

00:28:17.649 --> 00:28:22.429
BILL B.: -- because they wouldn't tell you.

00:28:22.449 --> 00:28:25.449
That might do worse things. You couldn't go to the brokers

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:25.750
because

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:28.730
the brokers are so busy preening themselves in front of

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:29.769
Gazprom to get

00:28:29.789 --> 00:28:32.539
corporate finance work that the last thing they would do was

00:28:32.179 --> 00:28:33.179
tell

00:28:33.179 --> 00:28:35.519
anybody how much stealing was going on.

00:28:35.539 --> 00:28:38.039
But I learned something as a consultant at the Boston

00:28:37.159 --> 00:28:38.159
Consulting Group

00:28:38.159 --> 00:28:40.909
which was if you wanna find out the answer to something

00:28:40.849 --> 00:28:41.849
where it's not

00:28:41.849 --> 00:28:44.710
written down somewhere, you just go and interview people.

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:47.730
That's what the consultants do for any of you who are

00:28:47.059 --> 00:28:48.059
thinking about

00:28:48.059 --> 00:28:51.849
consulting. And so I said, let's make a list of all the

00:28:51.869 --> 00:28:52.869
people who

00:28:52.869 --> 00:28:55.369
know about stealing at Gazprom and just ask them to

00:28:55.029 --> 00:28:56.029
breakfast, lunch,

00:28:56.029 --> 00:28:58.529
dinner, tea, coffee, and see what we can learn from

00:28:58.049 --> 00:28:59.049
interviewing them.

00:28:59.049 --> 00:29:01.549
We didn't know whether anyone would accept our invitations

00:29:00.919 --> 00:29:01.919
or tell us

00:29:01.919 --> 00:29:03.339
anything but why not try?

00:29:03.359 --> 00:29:07.450
So we set up about 40 of these meals and most people

00:29:07.470 --> 00:29:08.470
accepted the

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:09.470
invitation.

00:29:09.009 --> 00:29:11.439
Why not?

00:29:11.459 --> 00:29:13.959
And we discovered something very interesting is that in the

00:29:13.139 --> 00:29:14.139
communist

00:29:14.139 --> 00:29:16.899
days the itchest person in Russia was maybe ten times richer

00:29:16.919 --> 00:29:17.919
than the

00:29:17.919 --> 00:29:22.579
poorest person. But by 1999, the richest person in Russia

00:29:22.599 --> 00:29:23.599
was, like,

00:29:23.599 --> 00:29:26.319
250,000 times richer than the poorest person.

00:29:26.339 --> 00:29:29.319
And that just poisoned the whole environment of the country.

00:29:29.339 --> 00:29:31.730
Everyone hated everybody else, they just hated the rich

00:29:31.750 --> 00:29:32.750
people, they

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:34.419
hated the people that stole.

00:29:34.439 --> 00:29:36.939
And so people were in these meetings were spilling these

00:29:36.939 --> 00:29:37.939
guts out to

00:29:37.939 --> 00:29:40.259
us about all the different scams they knew about.

00:29:40.279 --> 00:29:42.529
We were filling up notebooks with all these different

00:29:41.919 --> 00:29:42.919
allegations of

00:29:42.919 --> 00:29:43.919
scams.

00:29:43.490 --> 00:29:45.879
It was interesting, really interesting stuff that people

00:29:45.899 --> 00:29:46.899
were telling

00:29:46.899 --> 00:29:47.899
us.

00:29:47.369 --> 00:29:48.369
Writing it all down.

00:29:48.199 --> 00:29:50.949
We filled up a whole notebook with these allegations but how

00:29:50.909 --> 00:29:51.909
do you

00:29:51.909 --> 00:29:53.659
know any of the stuff is true?

00:29:53.369 --> 00:29:55.230
A lot of sour grapes.

00:29:55.250 --> 00:29:58.990
Now, Russia has one other great, interesting anomaly which

00:29:59.009 --> 00:30:00.009
is it's got

00:30:00.009 --> 00:30:02.679
to be the most bureaucratic country in the world.

00:30:02.699 --> 00:30:04.949
Everything that ever happens in Russia get filed in

00:30:04.429 --> 00:30:05.429
quadruplicate in

00:30:05.429 --> 00:30:06.439
four different ministries.

00:30:06.459 --> 00:30:09.459
If you go to the bathroom you write it down, some ministry,

00:30:08.709 --> 00:30:09.709
like,

00:30:09.709 --> 00:30:12.419
registers it.

00:30:12.439 --> 00:30:16.059
And so -- and what's interesting is that you can go and just

00:30:16.079 --> 00:30:17.079
ask for

00:30:17.079 --> 00:30:18.079
the information.

00:30:17.579 --> 00:30:20.079
It's just a question of going to the ministry.

00:30:19.829 --> 00:30:23.579
And so one of my guys, one of the guys who works for me, my

00:30:23.079 --> 00:30:24.079
head of

00:30:24.079 --> 00:30:25.829
research, started going around to different ministries

00:30:25.329 --> 00:30:26.329
picking up

00:30:26.329 --> 00:30:29.079
databases on different things and we were able to take these

00:30:28.349 --> 00:30:29.349
databases

00:30:29.349 --> 00:30:32.099
we got from the ministries and cross reference them with all

00:30:31.599 --> 00:30:32.599
the stuff

00:30:32.599 --> 00:30:36.119
we learned in these breakfasts, lunches, and dinners, and we

00:30:36.139 --> 00:30:37.139
learned

00:30:37.139 --> 00:30:40.419
exactly how much had been stolen from Gazprom, by who, in

00:30:40.439 --> 00:30:42.450
what way.

00:30:42.470 --> 00:30:47.169
And basically what we learned was that nine individuals from

00:30:47.189 --> 00:30:51.359
management of Gazprom had stolen an oil company the size of

00:30:51.379 --> 00:30:52.379
Exxon out

00:30:52.379 --> 00:30:53.379
of Gazprom.

00:30:52.879 --> 00:30:56.329
That's pretty dramatic.

00:30:56.349 --> 00:30:58.159
That's the size of Kuwait.

00:30:58.179 --> 00:31:01.179
The oil reserves the size of Kuwait had been stolen out of

00:30:59.899 --> 00:31:00.899
Gazprom.

00:31:00.899 --> 00:31:09.839
But we also learned that the oil company the size of Kuwait

00:31:09.859 --> 00:31:10.859
is only

00:31:10.859 --> 00:31:12.359
9 percent of Gazprom's reserves.

00:31:12.359 --> 00:31:14.399
91 percent was still there.

00:31:14.419 --> 00:31:18.409
So what do you do if the market's pricing as if there's 99.

00:31:18.429 --> 00:31:19.429
97 percent

00:31:19.429 --> 00:31:25.069
discount and you've just discovered that really there should

00:31:25.089 --> 00:31:26.089
only be a

00:31:26.089 --> 00:31:27.089
10 percent discount?

00:31:26.839 --> 00:31:29.339
You go and buy the hell out of the thing.

00:31:28.899 --> 00:31:30.399
And that's what we did.

00:31:30.009 --> 00:31:32.960
We made Gazprom our single largest investment.

00:31:32.980 --> 00:31:35.980
That's usually where a fund manager would stop but we said

00:31:35.109 --> 00:31:36.109
to

00:31:36.109 --> 00:31:38.359
ourselves, this is just so morally outrageous what these

00:31:38.109 --> 00:31:39.109
guys is done

00:31:39.109 --> 00:31:40.359
and it's so obvious.

00:31:40.109 --> 00:31:42.109
Let's share this information with the world.

00:31:41.959 --> 00:31:42.159
&gt;&gt;

00:31:42.159 --> 00:31:43.159
[Laughing]

00:31:42.159 --> 00:31:51.399
BILL B.: And so we did.

00:31:51.419 --> 00:31:52.919
We broke it into seven chapters.

00:31:52.599 --> 00:31:55.599
I gave a chapter to the Financial Times, a chapter to Wall

00:31:54.849 --> 00:31:55.849
Street

00:31:55.849 --> 00:31:58.599
Journal, a chapter to the New York Times, Business Week, and

00:31:58.089 --> 00:31:59.089
each of

00:31:59.089 --> 00:32:04.359
them wrote a story, and boy did that set the Moscow night on

00:32:04.379 --> 00:32:05.379
fire.

00:32:05.379 --> 00:32:05.579
&gt;&gt;

00:32:05.379 --> 00:32:06.379
[Laughing]

00:32:05.379 --> 00:32:11.019
BILL B.: There are parliamentary hearings, there were

00:32:11.039 --> 00:32:12.039
shareholder

00:32:12.039 --> 00:32:14.039
votes, there was articles, more articles, there were

00:32:13.789 --> 00:32:14.789
hearings,

00:32:14.789 --> 00:32:18.179
investigations.

00:32:18.199 --> 00:32:23.000
And about seven months later, Putin, who had been President

00:32:23.019 --> 00:32:25.500
in 1999,

00:32:25.519 --> 00:32:30.139
stepped in, fired the head of Gazprom, put a new guy in and

00:32:30.159 --> 00:32:31.159
said his

00:32:31.159 --> 00:32:33.859
responsibility was to recover the lost assets and not let

00:32:33.879 --> 00:32:34.879
any more

00:32:34.879 --> 00:32:38.449
Gazprom assets leave the company. And from the moment that

00:32:38.469 --> 00:32:39.469
we started

00:32:39.469 --> 00:32:43.149
this thing until 2005 the stock price was up a hundred

00:32:43.169 --> 00:32:44.169
times.

00:32:44.169 --> 00:32:44.369
&gt;&gt;

00:32:44.169 --> 00:32:45.169
[Laughing]

00:32:44.169 --> 00:32:52.569
BILL B.: We got so excited by this that we started doing it

00:32:52.589 --> 00:32:54.199
elsewhere.

00:32:54.219 --> 00:32:56.719
We did it at the Unified Energy Systems, the National

00:32:55.839 --> 00:32:56.839
Electricity

00:32:56.839 --> 00:33:01.129
Company, we did it at Spear Bank, the National Savings Bank,

00:33:01.149 --> 00:33:02.149
we did it

00:33:02.149 --> 00:33:08.059
at Sergey Nevta Gas, and it was working like a charm.

00:33:08.079 --> 00:33:10.579
Not everything was remarkable as Gazprom but it was all

00:33:10.519 --> 00:33:11.519
pretty

00:33:11.519 --> 00:33:13.490
amazing.

00:33:13.509 --> 00:33:16.669
The fund went up something like 40 times from -- we went

00:33:16.689 --> 00:33:17.689
from a

00:33:17.689 --> 00:33:20.519
hundred million to more than 4 billion.

00:33:20.539 --> 00:33:26.439
I became the largest investor in Russia.

00:33:26.459 --> 00:33:29.439
It was just amazing.

00:33:29.459 --> 00:33:33.019
And now I'm gonna show you a video for the last ten minutes

00:33:33.039 --> 00:33:34.039
of what

00:33:34.039 --> 00:33:39.029
happened next because the video is better than I could ever

00:33:39.049 --> 00:33:40.119
do it.

00:33:40.139 --> 00:33:40.339
&gt;&gt;

00:33:40.139 --> 00:33:41.139
[Laughing].

00:33:40.139 --> 00:33:41.889
&gt;&gt; It's the true kafka-esque situation.

00:33:41.689 --> 00:33:45.029
It's black is white, white is black, it's the most

00:33:45.049 --> 00:33:46.049
unbelievable thing

00:33:46.049 --> 00:33:47.049
you could ever imagine.

00:33:46.769 --> 00:33:49.269
It's one thing to be victimized of a crime.

00:33:48.859 --> 00:33:51.859
It's another thing to be charged for the crime you're

00:33:50.779 --> 00:33:51.779
convicted of.

00:33:51.779 --> 00:33:55.569
&gt;&gt; The following is a story so extraordinary you couldn't

00:33:55.589 --> 00:33:56.589
make it up,

00:33:56.589 --> 00:34:00.230
a story about the risks of investing in Russia today.

00:34:00.250 --> 00:34:04.470
A story about how companies are stolen, criminals take over

00:34:04.490 --> 00:34:05.490
banks, and

00:34:05.490 --> 00:34:10.019
murderers dictate to judges, about how politicians pay lip

00:34:10.039 --> 00:34:11.039
service to

00:34:11.039 --> 00:34:14.569
cleaning up the system but in fact do nothing or actively

00:34:14.589 --> 00:34:15.589
assist the

00:34:15.589 --> 00:34:16.589
criminals.

00:34:16.250 --> 00:34:20.039
It's a story whose twists and turns lead down a dark trail

00:34:20.059 --> 00:34:21.059
of

00:34:21.059 --> 00:34:25.880
corruption, violence, and the theft of $230 million from the

00:34:25.900 --> 00:34:26.900
Russian

00:34:26.900 --> 00:34:28.130
people.

00:34:28.150 --> 00:34:31.670
Crimes like the one we're about to describe happen in Russia

00:34:31.690 --> 00:34:32.690
every

00:34:32.690 --> 00:34:33.690
day.

00:34:33.179 --> 00:34:41.480
And this is how it can happen.

00:34:41.500 --> 00:34:44.369
&gt;&gt; Hermitage Capital Management is an investment firm that

00:34:44.389 --> 00:34:45.389
started in

00:34:45.389 --> 00:34:48.880
1996 to focus on the Russian equity market and I founded it

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:49.900
in

00:34:49.900 --> 00:34:52.400
partnership with the late Edmond Safra and we were the

00:34:52.059 --> 00:34:53.059
largest foreign

00:34:53.059 --> 00:34:54.679
portfolio investor in Russia.

00:34:54.699 --> 00:34:56.949
Everything had been going beautifully with the business, we

00:34:56.829 --> 00:34:57.829
had been

00:34:57.829 --> 00:35:00.329
growing the fund, we had been making money for our

00:35:00.119 --> 00:35:01.119
investors, we had

00:35:01.119 --> 00:35:03.369
been stamping out corruption in the companies we invested

00:35:03.179 --> 00:35:04.179
in.

00:35:04.179 --> 00:35:07.059
But as often happens when you step on enough toes,

00:35:07.079 --> 00:35:08.079
eventually

00:35:08.079 --> 00:35:11.299
people -- certain people -- get angry with you.

00:35:11.319 --> 00:35:14.779
&gt;&gt; On November the 13th, 2005, when he was returning home to

00:35:14.799 --> 00:35:15.799
Moscow

00:35:15.799 --> 00:35:19.309
from a business trip, Bill Browder was refused entry at the

00:35:19.329 --> 00:35:20.329
airport.

00:35:20.329 --> 00:35:23.329
Later he was told by the Russian authorities that he'd been

00:35:23.279 --> 00:35:24.279
banned

00:35:24.279 --> 00:35:28.019
from the country on the grounds of national security.

00:35:28.039 --> 00:35:31.279
&gt;&gt; I then tried using every friend and contact I had to get

00:35:31.299 --> 00:35:32.299
back into

00:35:32.299 --> 00:35:33.299
Russia.

00:35:32.989 --> 00:35:34.380
I had an interesting opportunity.

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:37.969
I was tending the world economic forum in Davos in January

00:35:37.989 --> 00:35:39.719
2007.

00:35:39.739 --> 00:35:42.610
Dmitri Medvedev, who's now the President who was then the

00:35:42.630 --> 00:35:43.630
First Deputy

00:35:43.630 --> 00:35:47.380
Prime Minister was making his official foreign debut and at

00:35:47.400 --> 00:35:48.400
the forum

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:51.400
I had an opportunity to have a brief conversation with him

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:52.420
where I

00:35:52.420 --> 00:35:54.980
asked him for help in restoring my visa.

00:35:55.000 --> 00:35:58.250
And he was responsive, he said he would help, he asked for a

00:35:58.250 --> 00:35:59.250
copy of

00:35:59.250 --> 00:36:00.329
my visa application.

00:36:00.349 --> 00:36:03.099
I thought with the help of the First Deputy Prime Minister

00:36:02.819 --> 00:36:03.819
my visa

00:36:03.819 --> 00:36:06.259
would be approved in no time at all.

00:36:06.279 --> 00:36:09.079
&gt;&gt; What happened next was very different from what Bill had

00:36:09.099 --> 00:36:10.099
expected.

00:36:10.099 --> 00:36:12.599
Lieutenant Colonel Artem Kuznetsov of the tax crimes unit of

00:36:11.849 --> 00:36:12.849
the

00:36:12.849 --> 00:36:15.599
Moscow Ministry of the Interior who, a few weeks after Bill

00:36:14.849 --> 00:36:15.849
Browder's

00:36:15.849 --> 00:36:18.099
appeal to the first deputy prime minister Kuznetsovto re-

00:36:17.599 --> 00:36:18.599
issue his

00:36:18.599 --> 00:36:19.599
visa, telephoned Hermitage.

00:36:19.349 --> 00:36:21.599
&gt;&gt; Lieutenant Colonel Artem Kuznetsov called up my head of

00:36:20.849 --> 00:36:21.849
research.

00:36:21.849 --> 00:36:22.049
&gt;&gt;

00:36:21.849 --> 00:36:22.849
[Speaking in native language]

00:36:21.849 --> 00:36:24.599
&gt;&gt; And he said, I understand that the CEO of your company

00:36:24.099 --> 00:36:25.099
wishes to

00:36:25.099 --> 00:36:28.099
get a visa to visa our country and I'm responsible for

00:36:27.349 --> 00:36:28.349
writing the

00:36:28.349 --> 00:36:41.049
report.

00:36:41.069 --> 00:36:44.069
Before I write the report, I'd like to have an informal

00:36:43.710 --> 00:36:44.710
meeting, show

00:36:44.710 --> 00:36:46.710
you a few papers, ask a few questions.

00:36:46.639 --> 00:36:49.389
Depending on how you behave and what you provide in this

00:36:49.009 --> 00:36:50.009
meeting will

00:36:50.009 --> 00:36:54.529
be the determining factor in whether the visa gets issued or

00:36:54.549 --> 00:36:55.549
not.

00:36:55.549 --> 00:36:58.369
&gt;&gt; Hermitage rejected this extortion attempt and refused to

00:36:58.389 --> 00:36:59.389
talk with

00:36:59.389 --> 00:37:03.529
Kuznetsov but Kuznetsov had he has own plans.

00:37:03.549 --> 00:37:07.579
On July the fourth, 2007, Lieutenant Colonel Kuznetsov led a

00:37:07.599 --> 00:37:08.599
team of

00:37:08.599 --> 00:37:12.690
25 officers in a raid on the Hermitage Moscow offices taking

00:37:12.710 --> 00:37:13.710
servers,

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:16.429
computers, and documents.

00:37:16.449 --> 00:37:19.449
The official reason he gave for this raid was that one of

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:19.940
the

00:37:19.940 --> 00:37:23.799
Hermitage companies, Kamaya, had supposedly underpaid

00:37:23.819 --> 00:37:24.819
dividend

00:37:24.819 --> 00:37:27.339
withholding tax.

00:37:27.359 --> 00:37:29.069
This was not true.

00:37:29.089 --> 00:37:32.420
Not only had Kamaya paid up its tax, but the Russian tax

00:37:32.440 --> 00:37:33.440
authorities

00:37:33.440 --> 00:37:37.230
certified in writing that it had overpaid them by as much as

00:37:37.250 --> 00:37:38.250
4 million

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:39.809
rubles.

00:37:39.829 --> 00:37:43.269
&gt;&gt; In addition to Kuznetsov raiding our offices he then

00:37:43.289 --> 00:37:44.289
joined an

00:37:44.289 --> 00:37:47.289
ongoing raid on the same day at our offices at the offices

00:37:47.299 --> 00:37:48.299
of our law

00:37:48.299 --> 00:37:49.299
firm, Firestone Duncan.

00:37:49.049 --> 00:37:51.549
One of their young lawyers protested instead of trying to

00:37:50.909 --> 00:37:51.909
explain

00:37:51.909 --> 00:37:53.319
whatever their legal logic was.

00:37:53.339 --> 00:37:55.839
They took him into a conference room, beat him viciously,

00:37:55.440 --> 00:37:56.440
arrested him

00:37:56.440 --> 00:37:59.440
for resisting the search, he was fined 15,000 rubles to get

00:37:59.069 --> 00:38:00.069
out of

00:38:00.069 --> 00:38:03.069
jail, and then he was in the hospital for two weeks having

00:38:02.489 --> 00:38:03.489
to recover

00:38:03.489 --> 00:38:04.730
from his beating.

00:38:04.750 --> 00:38:07.659
&gt;&gt; In fact, Kuznetsov's raids were the first part of the

00:38:07.679 --> 00:38:08.679
criminal's

00:38:08.679 --> 00:38:12.190
plan: A fishing expedition to find out the amount of

00:38:12.210 --> 00:38:13.210
Hermitage's

00:38:13.210 --> 00:38:15.949
assets and in which banks they were held.

00:38:15.969 --> 00:38:20.039
At the same time, Kuznetsov seized all of Hermitage's

00:38:20.059 --> 00:38:21.059
corporate

00:38:21.059 --> 00:38:22.360
documents and certificates.

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:26.630
These official documents would be used by the criminals to

00:38:26.650 --> 00:38:27.650
steal the

00:38:27.650 --> 00:38:29.869
Hermitage companies.

00:38:29.889 --> 00:38:33.960
What happened next occurred without Hermitage knowing.

00:38:33.980 --> 00:38:38.019
The criminals went to the Russian courts in Kazan, St.

00:38:38.039 --> 00:38:39.039
